```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class MultipleChoiceDialogue {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] choices = {"Option 1", "Option 2", "Option 3"};
        int userChoice = -1;

        System.out.println("Choose an option:");
        for (int i = 0; i < choices.length; i++) {
            System.out.println((i + 1) + ". " + choices[i]);
        }

        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        while (userChoice < 0 || userChoice > choices.length) {
            System.out.print("Enter your choice (1-" + choices.length + "): ");
            userChoice = scanner.nextInt();
        }

        System.out.println("You chose: " + choices[userChoice - 1]);
    }
}
```

In this example, we create an array `choices` to store the options, and a variable `userChoice` to store the user's selection. We then use a loop to present the choices to the user and print them with numbers.

We use a `Scanner` to read the user's input and check if it's within the valid range. If the user enters an invalid choice, we print an error message and ask for input again.

Once the user makes a valid selection, we print the chosen option and the program ends. You can replace the `System.out.println("You chose: " + choices[userChoice - 1]);` line with your own logic to handle the chosen option in your game.

